  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
         <p>
      <em>Multinomial Coefficients</em>
    </p>
    <p>
      We saw in the in class examples that we if we have <m>n</m> objects,
      <m>n_{1}</m> of which are similar, <m>n_{2}</m> are similar, <m>n_{3}</m>
      are similar, .., <m>n_{s}</m> similar where
      <m>n_{1}+n_{2}+\cdots n_{s}=n</m> then the number of distinguishable
      permutations of the <m>n</m> objects are
    </p>
    </statement>
</exercise>
    <p>
      This is called a <alert>multinomial coefficient</alert> because it arises
      as the coefficient of <m>a_{1}^{n_1}a_{2}^{n_2}\cdots a_{s}^{n_s}</m> in
      the expansion of <m>(a_{1}+a_{2}+\cdots a_{s})^{n}</m>
    </p>
</page>
</worksheet>